GPT-6 Astra describes to me with the following
possible scenario:

    if (f.car instanceof Cons a && a.hash == 0) {
        /* schedule child... */
    }

The child is observed from the parent to be nonzero,
so scheduling is skipped.

Later, when we get to computing the actual hash
(forgetting that f.car is a Cons, treating it as
an opaque Datum object), Cons::hashCode recurses:

    ... + f.car.hashCode() ...

               |
               v

    int myHash = this.hash;
    if (myHash != 0) {
        return myHash;
    }

... and the child observes itself as to be zero.
(This can happen when another thread wrote the
child's nonzero hash, without synchronization
establishing a happens-before relationship to
our reads (in this scenario our thread has not
itself written the child's hash.))

Have this happen a few more times on a long-enough
list, and we get blown up the very StackOverflowError
that we've been trying to avoid.
